[size=5][b]Crimson Desert Telemetry 2.2.0[/b][/size]

[b]Live light, player, render-camera and ambient/sky telemetry for Crimson Desert, with local HTTP/WebSocket APIs, fullscreen light markers and a configurable 3D radar.[/b]

[b]New in 2.2:[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Lights all around you.[/b] Telemetry now reads the game's light list before the renderer discards what the current view cannot see. Lights behind the camera and out of view stay in the API, radar and markers. A fire bowl arrives as one light instead of many flickering particles.[/*]
[*][b]Which lights actually reach you.[/b] The game's own physics casts nine rays from the camera to every nearby light. A wall blocks all nine; a lantern cage blocks only some, so the lantern stays visible. The HUD dims blocked lights (F11 hides them) and the API reports clear / blocked / unknown per light. On by default.[/*]
[/list]

Inspect current lights from fires, candles, lanterns and other game sources: world position, linear HDR RGB, derived luminance, and direction/cone angle for recognized spotlights. Independent player position/root orientation and the native render camera are included. A separate grouped/EMA stream smooths lights without changing or removing the raw records. One ASI starts the local telemetry host automatically.

[url=https://youtu.be/eyRkkTXAU64]Watch the in-game demonstration[/url]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry/v2.0.0/media/screenshot1.jpg[/img]

[size=4][b]Light visibility: what it is and is not[/b][/size]

Visibility is a sampled collision estimate, not a measurement of how much light gets through. Nine rays go from the camera to fixed points around each light within the configured radius (100 game units around the player in the supplied INI), including lights behind the camera. Any free ray counts as clear; all nine blocked counts as blocked; anything missing, failed or older than 500 ms stays unknown and is never hidden. Thin gaps can make a light behind a fence or cage clear, and geometry without collision (foliage, some decorations) does not block. Up to 256 lights share 20 physics batches per second, so a crowded radius refreshes each light less often. No API record or RGB value is removed. Set [SourceVisibility] Enabled=0 to switch it off.

The complete source and preserved research are on [url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry]GitHub[/url]. Everyone is welcome to fork the repository, test alternatives and contribute improvements. Please keep evidence separated by game build, light type and exact player/light position.

[size=4][b]Install or upgrade[/b][/size]

Requires Windows x64, Microsoft .NET 8 ASP.NET Core Runtime (x64), and one x64 ASI loader installed for the game. Supports [b]Crimson Desert patch 2.03.02[/b] on Steam for Windows (EXE 1.0.0.2976, Steam build 25477059). Unknown executables are rejected; a game update can require a new version.

[list]
[*]Close Crimson Desert before installing, updating or removing the package.[/*]
[*]Back up useful INI preferences and disable/delete the previous Telemetry package in DMM.[/*]
[*]When upgrading through DMM 2.8.1, check bin64 for old Telemetry companions. Local tests showed that DMM can retain the two DLLs and two lowercase CFG files listed below.[/*]
[*]Delete only old CrimsonDesertTelemetry.Core.dll, crimson-desert-telemetry.dll, crimson-desert-telemetry.deps.cfg and crimson-desert-telemetry.runtimeconfig.cfg. Do not delete the ASI loader.[/*]
[*]Import and activate/deploy the complete ModManagers ZIP. Importing alone is insufficient. Keep all included files together.[/*]
[*]Launch the game and load a save. Restart after INI changes; hot-unloading is unsupported.[/*]
[/list]

The six runtime files beside the game EXE must come from the same version:

[code]
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.asi
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.Core.dll
CrimsonDesertTelemetry.ini
crimson-desert-telemetry.dll
crimson-desert-telemetry.deps.cfg
crimson-desert-telemetry.runtimeconfig.cfg
[/code]

Keep the .cfg names unchanged; renaming them to .json can make DMM interpret them as game patches. New INI keys in 2.2: [Lights] Upstream=1, [SourceVisibility] Enabled=1, [LightOverlay] Radius=100. Merge your preferences into the supplied INI. JSON Mod Manager and manual-loader lifecycle testing remain incomplete.

[size=4][b]Controls and defaults[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]F8:[/b] toggle the corner HUD and 3D radar.[/*]
[*][b]F9:[/b] toggle detailed diagnostics.[/*]
[*][b]F10:[/b] toggle fullscreen light markers.[/*]
[*][b]F11:[/b] hide or show lights whose nine visibility rays are all blocked (by default they are dimmed).[/*]
[*]Each key is configurable as a decimal Windows virtual-key code in the INI; use 0 to disable a shortcut.[/*]
[/list]

On the radar and markers, filled dots are lights the renderer uses in the current view; hollow rings are current lights it does not use there (not OFF). Detail cards show fire groups and renderer selection. Player/camera publication defaults to 60 Hz; native light capture to 20 Hz. Hiding the HUD does not stop telemetry. Automatic menu hiding is not implemented; use F8/F10. Research, console and explorer switches cannot be enabled through an old INI in this production build.

[size=4][b]HTTP and WebSocket access[/b][/size]

All routes are loopback-only JSON services:

[code]
HTTP snapshot: http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/snapshot
Health: http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/health
Snapshot schema: http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/schema
Raw WebSocket: ws://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/stream
Smoothed lights: http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/lights/smoothed
Smoothed WebSocket: ws://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/lights/smoothed/stream
Ambient/sky: http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/ambient
Ambient schema: http://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/ambient/schema
Ambient WebSocket: ws://127.0.0.1:27311/v1/ambient/stream
[/code]

Light snapshots use schema 1.6: lights.upstream lists all current engine lights with rendererSelected, and upstream and rendered lights carry sourceVisibility (clear / blocked / unknown, ray counts, measurement age). The smoothed feed is separate and groups nearby lights before applying a configurable 200 ms EMA; proximity grouping is not physical lamp identity. Ambient includes global sky coefficients and, when available, camera-local sky exposure and a derived local estimate. It is renderer telemetry, not a physical brightness measurement. [url=https://github.com/ZappendusterFX/CrimsonDesertTelemetry/blob/main/docs/API.md]API reference and client examples[/url]. The API exposes telemetry and does not control game actions.

[size=4][b]SDR and HDR[/b][/size]

The D3D12 UI supports SDR, HDR10 and FP16 scRGB, selected from the game's output. HdrPaperWhiteNits controls UI white brightness from 80 to 500 nits. The compositor blends the overlay without changing the game's HDR settings. Automated tests cover SDR/HDR10/scRGB; live HDR-display, frame-generation and AMD/Intel game acceptance remain pending. No NVIDIA, DLSS, Streamline, Nsight or PIX runtime dependency is required.

[size=4][b]What the measurements mean[/b][/size]

The upstream list covers the lights the game currently hands to its renderer, including behind the camera. It is not a persistent world registry; absence does not prove that a lamp is off. Authored, rendered and upstream arrays overlap and must not be summed. One lamp can produce several rendered records. Linear HDR RGB is renderer data, not lumens or final screen pixels: the values are linear and unclamped, so they routinely exceed 1.0 for bright sources and are not 0-255 colours. Tone map them before display. Fast movement can expose projection latency; displayed spotlight lengths are schematic.

The external host reads game memory; the ASI uses guarded renderer/D3D12 hooks, bounded GPU copies and the game's own physics queries. The production build contains only the mechanisms needed for the released features; larger diagnostics remain outside it.

